# LCU Companion Agent Guide

This is the proposed replacement project guide for future agents working on
`lcu-companion`.

## Mission and architecture

LCU Companion is a Tauri 2 desktop application for League of Legends. Svelte
renders the application, Tauri adapts commands, and a standalone Rust agent
owns long-running LCU synchronization, collection, persistence, and expensive
statistics work.

```text
Svelte presentation -> Tauri IPC facade -> authenticated agent IPC
                                             -> Rust domain logic
                                             -> SQLite and LCU
```

The migration is incremental. Existing Tauri services may still be active, but
new collection and statistics behavior belongs in the agent unless a
compatibility boundary requires otherwise.

## Repository map

```text
src/                         Svelte 5 + TypeScript frontend
src/lib/api/                 Typed Tauri/agent request wrappers
src/lib/components/          Presentation components
src-tauri/src/agent/         Standalone agent process and collector
src-tauri/src/commands/      Tauri command facades
src-tauri/src/services/      Existing LCU, cache, profile, and rune services
src-tauri/src/agent_supervisor.rs
                             Agent lifecycle and authenticated IPC
@llm/PLAN.md                 Architecture migration plan
@llm/docs/context/           Current state and handoffs
@llm/docs/db/                SQLite ownership and schema documentation
```

Read `@llm/docs/context/SESSION_HANDOFF_2026-10-11.md` before modifying the
agent.

The original session request and its exact collector log are preserved in the
handoff under **Original handoff request**. The immediate operational concern
is the delay after `backfill_complete=true` and `Reconciled 20 recent games`;
profile that pipeline before changing collection limits or concurrency.

## Agent process

The binary is `src-tauri/src/bin/lcu-companion-agent.rs`. Build it with:

```powershell
cargo build --manifest-path src-tauri\Cargo.toml --bin lcu-companion-agent
```

The supervisor normally launches
`src-tauri\target\debug\lcu-companion-agent.exe`. Restart Tauri after building.
`pnpm tauri dev` does not reliably rebuild an existing standalone executable.

The agent accepts `--port <port> --token <token>` and listens only on
`127.0.0.1`. Requests are newline-delimited JSON containing `token`, `command`,
and optional `args`.

## Database ownership

The agent owns:

```text
%LOCALAPPDATA%\lcu-companion\agent.db
%LOCALAPPDATA%\lcu-companion\global-aram.db
```

Do not add another writer. Personal facts and recent games belong in
`agent.db`; global ARAM Mayhem games, participants, discovered accounts, and
configuration belong in `global-aram.db`.

Use the shared connection helper. Preserve the busy timeout, WAL-compatible
configuration, short-lived connections, and short transactions. Do not perform
network calls or large JSON parsing inside write transactions. Synchronous
rusqlite work from async code belongs in bounded `spawn_blocking` tasks.

## Collection flow

The collector discovers credentials, identifies the current account,
reconciles recent summaries, queues missing details, fetches details with
bounded concurrency, writes facts, and updates state/logs. Global ARAM
collection additionally follows participant PUUIDs breadth-first until the
configured game limit is reached.

`Reconciled 20 recent games` describes the current recent-history window. It
does not establish that the account has only 20 games. Trace backfill cursors
and the detail queue before changing route parameters.

Every phase should log elapsed time, counts, queue depth, database duration,
and projection duration. Collection needs durable cursors and job checkpoints
so restarts resume completed work instead of repeating scans.

## Statistics flow

Statistics belong in Rust:

- read normalized facts
- compute WR, WPA, KDA, damage, gold, duration, and pick rate
- exclude Poro-Snax IDs `2050` and `2052`
- apply filters, deterministic sorting, and pagination
- enrich metadata through backend caches
- return render-ready DTOs

The frontend requests scope, kind, filters, sort, and page, then renders the
response. It must not rebuild global match objects or recalculate aggregates.
The current agent cache is short-lived and process-local; replace it with
revision-based invalidation or materialized projections in future optimization.

## Logging and safety

Use the structured logger:

```text
[UTC timestamp] [LEVEL] [source] message | context
```

Never log credentials, authorization headers, tokens, or sensitive raw LCU
payloads. Preserve explicit errors; do not return empty success-shaped data.

## Development rules

- Windows paths use backslashes.
- Use `pnpm`, not `npm`.
- Use lowercase CommunityDragon asset paths.
- Keep Svelte thin and domain calculations in Rust.
- Preserve backend cached assets and data URIs.
- Keep unrelated worktree changes intact.
- Do not use destructive Git commands.
- Do not run broad frontend validation proactively.

## Validation

```powershell
cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri\Cargo.toml
cargo fmt --manifest-path src-tauri\Cargo.toml -- --check
cargo build --manifest-path src-tauri\Cargo.toml --bin lcu-companion-agent
git diff --check
```

For runtime checks, launch the freshly built agent on an isolated loopback port,
send authenticated IPC requests, verify health/statistics/collector behavior,
and stop the test process afterward.

## Current priorities

1. Profile the delay after recent-history reconciliation.
2. Add durable backfill/account/detail checkpoints.
3. Ensure collector writes cannot starve normal reads.
4. Replace the temporary statistics cache with revision-aware projections.
5. Complete the Tauri-to-agent compatibility cutover after recovery behavior
   is verified.
